ntdll: RtlIsEcCode answers FALSE for an address above the user address space instead of faulting.

The ARM64EC code map has one bit per page of the user address space (alloc_arm64ec_map sizes it from
address_space_limit, 0x7fffffff0000 for 64-bit processes). RtlIsEcCode indexed it with any address, so an address
above that read past the map and faulted. The unwinder calls it through RtlLookupFunctionEntry for every frame it
walks; when it met a garbage return address (an x64 frame without unwind data, a value such as
0xffff000000000000 taken as the next pc), the fault was raised inside the dispatch, dispatched through the same
frames, met the same address and faulted again, about 280 times, until the thread's 1.5 MB stack ran out
("virtual_setup_exception stack overflow ... addr <ResetToConsistentStateImpl>"). Windows answers FALSE for such an
address and the walk goes on or stops cleanly. Microsoft Flight Simulator 2024's main thread hung this way 45 s
into every flight with FEX's store replay and smc-retire both on (hunt80, hunt83, hunt84, 2026-10-18; read from a
dump of the thread's stack), and once before on 2026-10-16 (boot73): the exception that started it is the game's
own, the endless recursion is this.

--- a/dlls/ntdll/signal_arm64ec.c
+++ b/dlls/ntdll/signal_arm64ec.c
@@ -1553,6 +1553,11 @@
 {
     const UINT64 *map = (const UINT64 *)NtCurrentTeb()->Peb->EcCodeBitMap;
     ULONG_PTR page = ptr / page_size;
+
+    /* Highball: the map covers the user address space only (unix/virtual.c's address_space_limit). Above it no
+     * address is EC code, as on Windows; reading the map there faulted, and when the unwinder met a garbage return
+     * address that fault was dispatched through the same unwind and faulted again until the stack ran out. */
+    if (ptr >= 0x7fffffff0000) return FALSE;
     return (map[page / 64] >> (page & 63)) & 1;
 }
 
